samples the posterior with a Gibbs sampler (Code S1). Here it is computed
exactly instead: given \code{z}, the integrals over \code{phi} and \code{lambda} are Beta
functions, so the posterior of \code{z} is a finite set of weights, and the
posteriors of \code{phi} and \code{lambda} are mixtures of Beta distributions.

The published numbers come from Code S1. Its \code{lambda} update,
\code{Beta(a_lambda + S, b_lambda + z - S)}, counts the extinction period
itself as a non-detection, one period more than the likelihood above. The
\code{z} update has no such term. The sampler is still a valid Gibbs sampler:
the extra factor \code{(1 - lambda)} is the same for every \code{z}, so it targets
the model above with the prior \code{lambda ~ Beta(a_lambda, b_lambda + 1)}.
\code{formulation = "published"} (the default) computes that posterior and
reproduces the paper's results. \code{formulation = "equations"} uses the
likelihood as printed in the paper.

\strong{Non-constant model} (\code{model = "nonconstant"}, Code S2). The population
size is \code{N[t] = exp(r (t - 1))}, with \code{N[1] = 1} fixed because \code{N[1]} and
\code{delta} are not separately identifiable. Detection is
\verb{lambda[t] = 1 - exp(-delta N[t])} (equation 3) and extinction is
\verb{logit(phi[t]) = eps0 - eps1 log(N[t])} (equation 4). The priors are
uniform on \code{prior_growth}, \code{prior_delta}, \code{prior_eps0} and \code{prior_eps1}.
Each iteration of the Metropolis-within-Gibbs sampler of Code S2 is ported
as published:
\enumerate{
\item impute \code{z} from its conditional distribution;
\item take a log-normal random-walk step for \code{delta} (sd 0.5);
\item take a joint step for \code{eps0} (normal, sd 1.5) and \code{eps1} (log-normal,
